### One important kubeconfig issue afterward

The downloaded kubeconfig often contains:

```yaml
server: https://127.0.0.1:6443
```

That works **on `master01`**, but not from your jump/Ansible controller.

After fetching it, you need the server field to point to a reachable control-plane address or preferably your future HA endpoint.

For now that could be:

```yaml
server: https://10.174.161.22:6443
```

Eventually, for HA, I would prefer:

```yaml
server: https://<RKE2-MGMT-VIP>:6443
```

and that VIP/DNS name should also be in the RKE2 `tls-san`.

A good Ansible pattern is:

```yaml
- name: Download kubeconfig to Ansible controller
  ansible.builtin.fetch:
    src: /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml
    dest: "{{ playbook_dir }}/../kubeconfig/rke2.yaml"
    flat: true
  run_once: true
  when: inventory_hostname == groups['masters'][0]

- name: Replace localhost API endpoint in downloaded kubeconfig
  ansible.builtin.replace:
    path: "{{ playbook_dir }}/../kubeconfig/rke2.yaml"
    regexp: 'https://127\.0\.0\.1:6443'
    replace: 'https://10.174.161.22:6443'
  delegate_to: localhost
  become: false
  run_once: true
```

The distinction is important:

```text
fetch kubeconfig:
remote master → controller
become: yes

edit downloaded kubeconfig:
controller → controller
delegate_to: localhost
become: no
```

